Fillets: a blend rolling on an edge whose contact on its face leaves the
face before the end of the spine is walked on, still rolling on the edge,
and cut by the face at the end (mitcad#237, with 0170, as 0074 does for
blends between two surfaces).

The case: a round pocket cut into a post, its floor a lens between the
pocket's wall and the post's rim narrower than the radius; rounding the
floor's edge at the wall, the ball touches the wall and rolls on the rim.
Near the lens's tips the ball's contact on the wall leaves the wall across
the vertical edge where wall and post meet, while the ball still rolls on
the rim. Before 0170 OCCT's sections lay in the guide's plane and reached
the tips; with the rolling ball the stripe stops at that edge, short of
the tip, and the corner at the tip intersected an extension of the blend
with the post's side: invalid (a wire intersecting itself on the post's
face, an edge whose range lies beyond its vertices).

- ChFi3d_Builder::RolledCutToEnd, called by PerformSetOfSurf for an end of
  an open spine where WalkedCutToEnd does not apply: where the stripe's
  end blend rolls on an edge Er on one side and its contact on face N
  leaves N across an edge E into the face B beyond Er, which holds the end
  vertex V, the blend is walked on from the stripe's end section with N's
  surface unbounded, still rolling on Er, until the ball reaches B (the
  walk leaves Er for B, or stops where the ball is tangent to B). That
  piece is cut by B (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece): its side on N becomes the cut
  of the blend by B, from the point on E to the point Q on Er where the
  blend ends in a point; its other side rolls on Er. Er's point at Q has
  the transition EXTERNAL: Er leaves B's boundary on both sides of Q (the
  blend's contact on Er bounds B towards the stripe, the cut part of B
  towards V). The end blend's contact curves no longer run past its end
  (where it was extended for the face at the end to cut it). The spine's
  end becomes a free boundary with a singular extremity: no corner at V.
